== Chapter VIII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[#emph[The Second general Rule proposed. Without universal Sincerity for the mortifying of every Lust, no Lust will be Mortified. Partial Mortification always from a corrupt Principle. Perplexity of Temptation from a Lust, oft-times a chastening for other Negligences.]]
]

==== Second Rule: Sincerity in a Universal Obedience

#smallcaps[THE] Second Principle, which to this purpose I shall propose, is this, #emph[Without Sincerity and Diligence in an Universality of Obedience, there is no Mortification of any one perplexing Lust to be obtained.] The other was to the #emph[Person,] this to the thing itself. I shall a little explain this Position.

A man finds any Lust to bring him into the Condition formerly described, it is powerful, strong, tumultuating, #emph[leads captive,] vexes, disquiets, takes away #emph[peace]\; He is not able to #emph[bear it,] wherefore he sets himself #emph[against] it, prays against it, #emph[groans] under it, #emph[sighs] to be delivered, but in the mean time, perhaps in other Duties, in constant Communion with God, in #emph[Reading, Prayer] and #emph[Meditation,] in other #emph[ways] that are not of the same kind with the #emph[Lust] wherewith he is troubled, he is #emph[loose] and #emph[negligent.] Let not that man think that ever he shall arrive to the #emph[Mortification] of the Lust he is #emph[perplexed withal.] This is a #emph[Condition] that not seldom befalls men in their #emph[Pilgrimage.] The #emph[Israelites] under a sense of their Sin, drew #emph[nigh] to God with much diligence and earnestness, with #emph[Fasting] and #emph[Prayer.] Isa 58 many Expressions are made of their #emph[Earnestness] in the work, v. 2. #emph[They seek me daily, and delight to know my Ways, they ask of me the Ordinances of Justice, they delight in approaching unto God.] But God rejects all; their #emph[Fast] is a Remedy that will not #emph[heal] them, and the Reason given of it, vv. 5, 6, 7 is, because they were #emph[particular in this Duty.] They attended diligently to #emph[that,] but in others were negligent and careless. He that hath a #emph[running sore] (it is the Scripture Expression) upon him, arising from an #emph[ill habit] of Body contracted by Intemperance and ill Diet; let him apply himself with what #emph[diligence] and skill he can, to the #emph[cure] of his #emph[sore,] if he leave the #emph[general] habit of his #emph[body] under Distempers, his #emph[labor] and #emph[travail] will be in vain. So will his #emph[attempts] be, that shall endeavor to stop a #emph[bloody issue] of Sin, and #emph[filth] in his Soul, and is not #emph[equally] careful of his universal #emph[spiritual temperature,] and Constitution. For,

1\. This kind of Endeavor for #emph[Mortification,] proceeds from a #emph[corrupt Principle,] Ground and Foundation, so that it will never #emph[proceed] to a good Issue. The true and acceptable #emph[Principles] of Mortification shall be afterward insisted on. #emph[Hatred] of Sin as Sin, not only as #emph[galling] or #emph[disquieting,] sense of the Love of Christ in the Cross lies at the #emph[bottom] of all true #emph[spiritual] Mortification. Now it is certain, that that which I speak of proceeds from #emph[self-love.] Thou settest thyself with all #emph[diligence] and earnestness to #emph[mortify] such a Lust or Sin; what is the Reason of it? It #emph[disquiets] thee, it hath taken away thy #emph[peace,] it #emph[fills] thy heart with sorrow and trouble, and fear, thou hast #emph[no rest] because of it; Yea but Friend, thou hast #emph[neglected Prayer] or #emph[Reading,] thou hast #emph[been] vain and #emph[loose] in thy Conversation in other things that have not been of the same #emph[nature] with that #emph[lust] wherewith thou art perplexed; These are no less sins and evils, than those under which thou #emph[groanest]\; Jesus Christ #emph[bled] for Them also; Why dost thou not set thyself against them also? If thou #emph[hatedst] sin #emph[as] Sin, #emph[every evil way,] thou wouldst be no less #emph[watchful] against every thing that #emph[grieves] and disquiets the #emph[Spirit] of God, than against that which #emph[grieves] and disquiets thine own Soul. It is evident that thou contendest against #emph[sin,] merely because of thy own #emph[trouble] by it. Would thy #emph[Conscience] be quiet under it, thou would'st let it alone. Did it not disquiet thee, it should not be disquieted by thee. Now, canst thou think that God will set in with such #emph[hypocritical] Endeavors; that #emph[ever his Spirit] will bear #emph[witness] to the #emph[treachery] and Falsehood of thy Spirit? Dost thou think he will #emph[ease] thee of that which #emph[perplexeth] Thee, that thou mayst be at liberty to that which no less #emph[grieves] Him? No, (says God,) here is #emph[one,] if he could be rid of this #emph[Lust] I should never hear of him more, let him wrestle with this, or he is lost. Let not any man think to do his #emph[own work,] that will not do God's. God's work consists in #emph[universal Obedience]\; To be freed of the #emph[present perplexity] is their own only. Hence is that of the Apostle, 2 Cor 7:1. #emph[Cleanse yourselves from all pollution of Flesh and Spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of the Lord.] If we will do #emph[any thing,] we must do #emph[all things.] So then, not only an intense Opposition to this or that peculiar #emph[lust,] but it is an #emph[universal humble] Frame and Temper of Heart, with watchfulness over every Evil, and for the performance of every #emph[Duty,] that is #emph[accepted.]

2\. How know'st thou but that God hath suffered the Lust wherewith thou hast been #emph[perplexed] to get #emph[strength] in thee, and #emph[power] over thee, to #emph[chasten] thee for thy other #emph[Negligences,] and common #emph[Lukewarmness] in walking before him; at least to #emph[awaken] thee to the #emph[consideration] of thy ways, that thou mayst make a #emph[thorough] work and change in thy #emph[course] of walking with him. The Rage and #emph[predominancy] of a particular Lust, is commonly the #emph[fruit] and issue of a careless, negligent #emph[course] in general; and that upon a double account.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(1) As its #emph[natural Effect,] if I may so say. Lust (as I showed) in general, lies in the Heart of every one, even the #emph[best,] whilst he lives; and think not that the #emph[Scripture] speaks in vain, that it is #emph[subtle, cunning, crafty]\; that it #emph[seduces, entices, fights, rebels.] Whilst a man keeps a diligent #emph[Watch] over his Heart, its Root and Fountain; whilst above #emph[all keepings, he keeps his Heart,] whence are the issues of Life and Death, Lust #emph[withers] and #emph[dies] in it. But if through #emph[Negligence] it makes an #emph[Eruption] any particular way, gets a #emph[passage] to the #emph[Thoughts] by the #emph[Affections,] and #emph[from] them, and #emph[by] them, perhaps breaks out into open sin in the #emph[Conversation,] the strength of it #emph[bears] that way it hath found out, and that way mainly it urgeth, until having got a #emph[passage,] it then vexes and disquiets, and is not easily to be restrained; Thus perhaps a man may be put to wrestle all his days in #emph[sorrow,] with that, which by a strict #emph[universal Watch] might easily have been prevented.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[(2) As I said, God often-times suffers it to #emph[chasten] our other Negligences; for as with Wicked men, he gives them up to #emph[one] Sin as the Judgment of another,#footnote[Rom 1:26.] a #emph[greater] for the #emph[punishment] of a #emph[less,] or one that will hold them more #emph[firmly] and securely, for that which they might have possibly obtained a Deliverance from: So even with his own, he may, he doth #emph[leave] them sometimes to some #emph[vexatious distempers,] either to prevent or cure some other Evil: So was the #emph[Messenger] of Satan let loose on #emph[Paul,] #footnote[2 Cor 12:7.] that he might not be lifted up #emph[through the abundance of spiritual Revelations.] Was it not a Correction to #emph[Peters vain] Confidence, that he was left to #emph[deny] his Master? Now if this be the #emph[state] and Condition of #emph[Lust] in its #emph[prevalency,] that God often-times suffers it so to prevail, at least to #emph[admonish] us, and to #emph[humble] us, perhaps to #emph[chasten] and correct us, for our general loose and careless walking, is it possible that the #emph[effect] should be removed, and the #emph[cause] continued; that the #emph[particular] Lust should be #emph[mortified,] and the #emph[general] Course be #emph[unreformed?] He then that would #emph[really, thoroughly,] and #emph[acceptably mortify] any disquieting #emph[Lust,] let him take care to be #emph[equally] diligent in All parts of #emph[Obedience]\; and know that every #emph[Lust,] every Omission of Duty, is #emph[burdensome] to God,#footnote[Isa 43:24.] though but one is so to Him. Whilst there abides a Treachery in the Heart to indulge to any Negligence in not pressing Universally to all Perfection in Obedience, the Soul is #emph[weak,] as not giving Faith its whole work; and #emph[selfish,] as considering more the Trouble of Sin, than the Filth and Guilt of it; and lives under a constant #emph[provocation] of God, so that it may not expect any comfortable Issue in any spiritual Duty that it doth undertake, much less in this under Consideration, which requires another Principle, and Frame of Spirit for its Accomplishment.]]
